1
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:04,850
- You're wrong. - No, I'm not.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:07,070
- Yes, you are. - No, I'm not.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:09,840
Settle this. Those little animated pictures on the Internet,

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,960
are they called "Gifs" or "Jifs"?

5
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:13,780
Well, the G stands for "Graphics."

6
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:15,640
That's a hard G, so I'd say "Gif."

7
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,320
W... The guy who invented it says it's "Jif."

8
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:22,250
I'm sorry, do you mean the guy or the juy?

9
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,160
Well, I'll give you three guesses

10
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:27,040
why I'm so irritated.

11
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,820
Something happened different from the way you wanted it.

12
00:00:31,870 --> 00:00:34,160
I guess news travels fast.

13
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:36,790
It's true, a select group of scientists

14
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:38,740
was invited to a weekend symposium

15
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:40,300
at a former home of Richard Feynman,

16
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:41,870
and I wasn't included.

17
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:44,010
Oh, Sheldon, I'm sure it's not because

18
00:00:44,010 --> 00:00:45,930
they don't think you're an elite scientist.

19
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:47,130
Yeah, I bet you anything

20
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:50,420
it's just 'cause you're a pain in the ass.

21
00:00:50,930 --> 00:00:53,620
You're just saying that to make me feel better.

22
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,680
Look, you can spend the rest of the day being bitter about this.

23
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,760
Agreed.

24
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:02,020
I was gonna say "or," but why bother?

25
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:29,480
Hey, Raj.

26
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:32,320
Hey, I got you a little gift.

27
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:34,790
Oh, that's a lot of Girl Scout Cookies.

28
00:01:34,790 --> 00:01:36,250
You know me. I'm from India.

29
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:38,790
I can't resist children begging.

30
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:41,900
So, how's it going with the title to the house?

31
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:43,030
Great, it's all done.

32
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:44,630
The lawyer tracked down my father

33
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:45,700
and got him to sign it over.

34
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,440
I didn't have to meet him, I didn't have to talk to him,

35
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,500
I don't even know where he is.

36
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,650
Wow, so you're not curious at all?

37
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:53,680
Nope.

38
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:55,520
What if he's in prison?

39
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:56,860
What if he's a spy?

40
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:59,370
What if he's in a Beatles cover band?

41
00:02:00,210 --> 00:02:02,790
I'm just saying, if he's got your nose and haircut,

42
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:05,520
he'd make a killer Ringo.

43
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:09,020
Thanks again for agreeing to do this.

44
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:11,530
Oh, it's cool; I've never been on a podcast before.

45
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:13,040
Well, you picked a good one to start.

46
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,450
Wil's had lots of great guests.

47
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:16,760
Jonathan Frakes, Brent Spiner,

48
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:19,160
Michael Dorn, Gates McFadden...

49
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,870
Those are Star Trek people.

50
00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:21,910
Yes!

51
00:02:21,910 --> 00:02:24,270
I only figured that out because I've never heard of any of them.

52
00:02:26,110 --> 00:02:27,130
I deserve that.

53
00:02:27,130 --> 00:02:30,200
I invited you on my show and I drove here.

54
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:31,510
Sorry.

55
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:34,080
Okay, so, this is basically gonna be just like a little talk show.

56
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:35,660
Uh, we're gonna take some calls,

57
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:37,350
we'll talk about what it was like on the set

58
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:39,020
of Serial Ape-ist 2.

59
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:39,930
It should be really fun.

60
00:02:39,930 --> 00:02:41,190
- This is exciting. - Yeah.

61
00:02:41,190 --> 00:02:42,470
So, how many people listen?

62
00:02:42,470 --> 00:02:43,680
Most people download it later,

63
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:45,990
but usually a few thousand people listen live.

64
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:47,880
What?! A few thousand people

65
00:02:47,880 --> 00:02:51,030
listen to you talk about nerd stuff?

66
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,270
Again, right in the ears,

67
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:56,440
straight to the feelings.

68
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:00,690
Can you please pass the salt?

69
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:03,010
Sure.

70
00:03:03,010 --> 00:03:05,280
It's not like I was invited to Richard Feynman's house

71
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:07,540
and have anything better to do.

72
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:10,570
Is this how the rest of the night's going to be?

73
00:03:10,570 --> 00:03:12,820
I don't know the future.

74
00:03:14,630 --> 00:03:16,100
Do you think there's a chance

75
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:17,970
that an asteroid could hit the Earth,

76
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:21,280
destroying Feynman's house and everyone in it?

77
00:03:22,530 --> 00:03:23,730
No, Sheldon.

78
00:03:23,730 --> 00:03:26,890
Then buckle up; you're in for a cranky night.

79
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:29,810
According to the codicil of the Relationship Agreement

80
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:31,530
which you insisted upon,

81
00:03:31,530 --> 00:03:34,380
we're not allowed to pout or be moody on date night.

82
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:38,920
You know I just put that in because of uterus stuff.

83
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:42,230
Well, it applies to you, too.

84
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:44,520
I was afraid you might bring this up,

85
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:48,300
so I have a work-around.

86
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:54,540
There you go.

87
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:03,240
As far as you're concerned, I'm smiling.

88
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:05,580
Although, I must admit, I'm smiling a little bit at the moment

89
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:08,680
because this loophole is so brilliant.

90
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:12,130
So, Bernadette, have you thought

91
00:04:12,130 --> 00:04:15,060
about how you're going to redecorate this place?

92
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:16,560
You know, I'm thinking

93
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:18,000
ripping up the carpets,

94
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,340
maybe lose the wallpaper,

95
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:20,880
all new light fixtures...

96
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:22,710
You know, if you knocked out this wall,

97
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:24,490
it would give you an open floor plan,

98
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:28,690
and then-- it's a little scary, but could be fun--

99
00:04:28,690 --> 00:04:31,260
indoor fire pit.

100
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:36,000
Hey, I grew up in this house, okay?

101
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,320
No one's knocking anything down.

102
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:39,970
Okay, okay.

103
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:40,810
When he's at Comic-Con,

104
00:04:40,810 --> 00:04:43,360
I'm bringing in a wrecking ball.

105
00:04:46,530 --> 00:04:47,760
Can I help you?

106
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:49,750
Hi. Are you Howard Wolowitz?

107
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:51,220
Yes.

108
00:04:51,220 --> 00:04:52,880
Um, this is a little weird,

109
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:55,090
but a lawyer was trying to contact my father,

110
00:04:55,090 --> 00:04:58,220
because his name was still on the title for this house.

111
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:00,310
W... uh, who's your father?

112
00:05:00,310 --> 00:05:02,750
Sam Wolowitz.

113
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:05,920
S-Sam Wolowitz is my father.

114
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:07,390
I know.

115
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:12,270
Well, wait, so if we have the same father...

116
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:13,790
I mean, are you saying

117
00:05:13,790 --> 00:05:15,550
you're my half-brother?

118
00:05:15,550 --> 00:05:17,230
I think so.

119
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:21,750
Bernadette, weird things are happening out here.

120
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:27,510
I think I started to suspect it was a bad movie

121
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:30,940
when I looked at the script and saw the title,

122
00:05:31,650 --> 00:05:35,970
Serial Ape-ist 2: Monkey See, Monkey Kill.

123
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:37,380
Uh, spoiler alert:

124
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:40,170
after the monkey sees, it kills.

125
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,370
I've just been handed a note.

126
00:05:44,370 --> 00:05:45,970
I'm going to read it.

127
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:49,610
"Wil, do you want more Diet Coke?

128
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:53,770
Also, we have juice."

129
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:57,190
I-I didn't want to interrupt.

130
00:05:58,830 --> 00:06:01,050
Uh, that voice you just heard belongs to Leonard,

131
00:06:01,050 --> 00:06:02,380
Penny's fiance.

132
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:04,730
Uh, Leonard, why don't you grab some headphones and join us?

133
00:06:04,730 --> 00:06:06,000
- Really? - Yeah.

134
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:07,150
Yeah, great.

135
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:08,970
So, while Leonard gets set up,

136
00:06:08,970 --> 00:06:10,370
let's take a call.

137
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:11,740
Hello, caller, you are on

138
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:14,560
with Penny and Wil from Serial Ape-ist 2.

139
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:15,770
I don't have a question.

140
00:06:15,770 --> 00:06:17,800
I just want to say I'm a big fan of the movie.

141
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:19,680
I've seen it, like, ten times.

142
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:21,970
Okay, well, I'll apologize for the first time,

143
00:06:21,970 --> 00:06:24,410
but the other nine are on you.

144
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:27,060
Thanks a lot, caller.

145
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:29,560
You know that the movie actually has a little bit of a cult following.

146
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:30,480
- Really? - Yeah.

147
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:31,930
I was at a science-fiction convention,

148
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:35,510
and I saw a woman dressed as your half-ape character.

149
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:38,870
Oh, if she was with an Indian guy dressed like a banana,

150
00:06:38,870 --> 00:06:41,850
that was just my friends, Howard and Raj.

151
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:45,530
All right, it's time for a very special guest caller,

152
00:06:45,530 --> 00:06:47,220
a friend of mine, who you probably know

153
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:49,290
as the director of such movies as Clerks,

154
00:06:49,290 --> 00:06:51,630
or from podcasts and books where he often reminds you that

155
00:06:51,630 --> 00:06:54,250
he's the guy who directed Clerks.

156
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:55,510
Hello, Kevin Smith.

157
00:06:55,510 --> 00:06:57,910
Hey, man, yo, is everything okay, Wil?

158
00:06:57,910 --> 00:06:59,440
'Cause it's been, like, two minutes

159
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:03,010
and you haven't even brought up Stand By Me.

160
00:07:03,970 --> 00:07:04,760
Hey, Kevin!

161
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:06,440
It's really exciting to talk to you.

162
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:07,590
It really is.

163
00:07:07,590 --> 00:07:09,700
Oh, you guys are very sweet. So, Penny, listen,

164
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:11,170
I saw your movie.

165
00:07:11,170 --> 00:07:13,130
Oh, wow. I wish it was better.

166
00:07:13,130 --> 00:07:14,080
Oh, don't worry about it.

167
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:16,510
Have you seen some of Kevin's films?

168
00:07:17,370 --> 00:07:19,940
You're cruisin' for a beatin', Wheaton.

169
00:07:21,530 --> 00:07:23,690
Anyway, man, I dug the ape movie, Penny.

170
00:07:23,690 --> 00:07:25,650
And I thought you were, like, really great in it.

171
00:07:26,490 --> 00:07:27,610
You know, I'm in the movie, too.

172
00:07:27,610 --> 00:07:29,620
Yeah, whatever. Penny!

173
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:32,640
Penny, how come you're not in more stuff, man?

174
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:34,090
I'd cast you in a minute.

175
00:07:34,090 --> 00:07:35,140
Seriously?

176
00:07:35,140 --> 00:07:35,860
Oh, yeah, man.

177
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,290
I'm actually in pre-production on a movie right now.

178
00:07:38,290 --> 00:07:40,640
Way different than anything I've ever done before.

179
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:43,340
It's called Clerks 3.

180
00:07:43,870 --> 00:07:45,820
You should come over and read for a part.

181
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:48,070
Oh, my, I would love that.

182
00:07:48,070 --> 00:07:49,620
You have a new job.

183
00:07:50,150 --> 00:07:51,440
Well, maybe I can do both.

184
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:53,480
I don't think you can do both.

185
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:56,210
I don't think I asked you.

186
00:07:56,210 --> 00:07:58,200
Yeah, you tell him, Penny.

187
00:07:59,370 --> 00:08:01,560
Stay out of it, Kevin Smith.

188
00:08:03,830 --> 00:08:05,770
Do you think there comes a point in life

189
00:08:05,770 --> 00:08:09,260
when it stops feeling bad to be left out of things?

190
00:08:09,260 --> 00:08:10,820
Probably not.

191
00:08:10,820 --> 00:08:15,070
It's an evolutionary advantage to be included in group activities.

192
00:08:15,070 --> 00:08:16,220
You know what?

193
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:18,210
I used to be a fan of evolution,

194
00:08:18,210 --> 00:08:19,690
but I've since evolved,

195
00:08:19,690 --> 00:08:21,960
and now I think it's dumb.

196
00:08:23,410 --> 00:08:25,280
Being left out is a terrible feeling.

197
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:27,680
No one understands that better than I do.

198
00:08:27,970 --> 00:08:29,760
Hey, look at that.

199
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:33,120
Even in your example, you're all by yourself.

200
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:36,480
When there was a lice epidemic at my school,

201
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:38,340
everybody got it except me.

202
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:40,990
I tried to fool everyone by sprinkling sugar in my hair,

203
00:08:40,990 --> 00:08:43,760
but I just got attacked by bees.

204
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:48,890
I can just picture them all right now at Feynman's house,

205
00:08:48,890 --> 00:08:51,320
probably discussing Schrodinger

206
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:54,750
and at the same time, not discussing Schrodinger.

207
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:56,080
See?

208
00:08:56,080 --> 00:08:59,170
They're missing out on hilarious jokes like that.

209
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:02,540
And at the same time, not.

210
00:09:03,430 --> 00:09:05,660
It reminds me of when my stupid brother and sister

211
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:07,380
would build forts in the living room

212
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:08,840
and wouldn't let me in.

213
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:11,160
I hated that so much.

214
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:13,080
You know,

215
00:09:13,790 --> 00:09:16,700
there's nothing I can do about getting you invited to the symposium,

216
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:18,780
but if you wanted...

217
00:09:19,390 --> 00:09:21,540
we could build a fort.

218
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:24,330
Isn't that a little juvenile?

219
00:09:24,330 --> 00:09:26,570
More juvenile than this?

220
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:30,790
I'll get the blankets.

221
00:09:30,790 --> 00:09:33,350
You Google how to have childlike fun.

222
00:09:35,930 --> 00:09:37,640
You know what, I'm being a bad host.

223
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:38,880
Let me get some more coffee.

224
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:39,610
Oh, let me do it, Howard.

225
00:09:39,610 --> 00:09:41,240
No, I got it.

226
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:45,220
So, Josh, what do you do?

227
00:09:45,220 --> 00:09:47,520
I'm studying oceanography down in San Diego.

228
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:49,300
Oh, how nice.

229
00:09:53,870 --> 00:09:56,550
I loved Finding Nemo.

230
00:09:56,940 --> 00:09:58,010
Enough chitchat.

231
00:09:58,010 --> 00:10:00,450
How do we know you are who you say you are?

232
00:10:01,230 --> 00:10:02,740
Why would I lie?

233
00:10:02,740 --> 00:10:05,250
Okay, you got me there.

234
00:10:06,310 --> 00:10:07,420
You here looking for money?

235
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:08,230
No.

236
00:10:08,230 --> 00:10:10,970
A kidney, cornea, piece of his liver?

237
00:10:10,970 --> 00:10:12,240
No.

238
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:14,150
You're in a Beatles cover band

239
00:10:14,150 --> 00:10:17,920
and you need Howard to replace your dad as Ringo.

240
00:10:19,210 --> 00:10:20,850
Why don't you help with the coffee?

241
00:10:20,850 --> 00:10:23,720
Okay, but something smells fishy.

242
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:26,520
And not just because you work around sea animals.

243
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:27,680
That actually sounds interesting,

244
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:29,760
and I'd like to learn more about it.

245
00:10:33,030 --> 00:10:34,340
Hey, you okay?

246
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:35,500
Not really.

247
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:37,970
This guy shows up out of the blue,

248
00:10:37,970 --> 00:10:39,800
and now I have a brother?

249
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:43,470
My father has another family?

250
00:10:43,470 --> 00:10:44,890
I get it.

251
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:46,470
What do you want to do?

252
00:10:46,470 --> 00:10:47,900
I don't know. I...

253
00:10:48,370 --> 00:10:50,280
I'd just like him to go away.

254
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:52,220
I can't deal with this.

255
00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:54,560
All right, I've got your back.

256
00:10:54,860 --> 00:10:56,500
Thank you. Come on.

257
00:10:58,610 --> 00:10:59,420
And I'd like to point out

258
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:02,360
this wall just provided a lot of privacy!

259
00:11:03,770 --> 00:11:05,360
I can't believe my brother's an astronaut.

260
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:07,030
That's amazing. What was it like?

261
00:11:07,030 --> 00:11:07,690
Listen, dude,

262
00:11:07,690 --> 00:11:08,710
it's time for you to hit the road.

263
00:11:08,710 --> 00:11:11,960
Hey, hey, the young man asked a good question.

264
00:11:17,690 --> 00:11:19,170
So, for those of you just tuning in,

265
00:11:19,170 --> 00:11:21,530
we are listening to a really fun fight

266
00:11:21,530 --> 00:11:22,820
- between my costar... - No, no, no.

267
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:23,780
We're not fighting; we're just...

268
00:11:23,780 --> 00:11:25,960
having a conversation.

269
00:11:26,450 --> 00:11:27,550
All right.

270
00:11:27,550 --> 00:11:30,210
We're listening to a really fun conversation

271
00:11:30,210 --> 00:11:32,880
between my costar from Serial Ape-ist 2

272
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:37,330
and her fiance, who doesn't believe women should have dreams.

273
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:41,340
Give me back that juice.

274
00:11:43,690 --> 00:11:45,180
What is the harm if I audition?

275
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:46,500
Well, what if you get it?

276
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:48,710
I don't know, I make a movie,

277
00:11:48,710 --> 00:11:50,050
become rich and famous,

278
00:11:50,050 --> 00:11:51,590
win an Oscar, a Golden Globe

279
00:11:51,590 --> 00:11:53,910
and live an incredibly wonderful life.

280
00:11:53,910 --> 00:11:56,420
From a Kevin Smith movie?

281
00:11:57,010 --> 00:11:59,140
Oh, I'm hanging up now.

282
00:11:59,950 --> 00:12:01,020
Don't you listen to him, buddy.

283
00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:01,840
You're awesome.

284
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:03,810
You're one of the greatest directors of our time.

285
00:12:03,810 --> 00:12:06,100
I don't have a part for you, Wheaton.

286
00:12:07,710 --> 00:12:09,970
And that was Kevin Smith.

287
00:12:10,730 --> 00:12:11,600
Thanks a lot.

288
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:13,870
I'm just trying to protect you.

289
00:12:14,130 --> 00:12:16,010
How many times did I see you get your heart broken

290
00:12:16,010 --> 00:12:17,500
trying to make it as an actress?

291
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:19,100
Okay, instead of protecting me, why don't you try

292
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:20,970
being excited when something good happens?

293
00:12:20,970 --> 00:12:23,470
I'm always excited for you!

294
00:12:23,470 --> 00:12:25,070
I'm excited that you found this new job

295
00:12:25,070 --> 00:12:26,810
where you're making decent money.

296
00:12:26,810 --> 00:12:28,740
Decent? I make twice what you make.

297
00:12:28,740 --> 00:12:30,450
Wait, twice?!

298
00:12:30,450 --> 00:12:32,030
Yeah.

299
00:12:32,910 --> 00:12:36,880
Like, "times two" twice?

300
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:38,620
For those of you at home, Leonard just found out

301
00:12:38,620 --> 00:12:42,320
his fiance makes way more money than he does.

302
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:44,580
Let's listen.

303
00:12:44,580 --> 00:12:46,770
I went to school for half my life.

304
00:12:46,770 --> 00:12:47,630
I have a doctorate.

305
00:12:47,630 --> 00:12:50,200
I'm still paying off college loans.

306
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:51,290
Well, how much do you owe?

307
00:12:51,290 --> 00:12:53,640
Maybe I can help you out.

308
00:12:55,390 --> 00:12:58,770
Wil, can we just turn off the podcast for a little bit?

309
00:12:58,770 --> 00:12:59,870
For those of you at home,

310
00:12:59,870 --> 00:13:02,500
I am shaking my head "no".

311
00:13:09,850 --> 00:13:11,440
How's it going, Sheldon?

312
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:13,230
Wonderful!

313
00:13:14,850 --> 00:13:17,070
I just finished hanging the lights.

314
00:13:17,070 --> 00:13:17,970
Can I come in?

315
00:13:17,970 --> 00:13:19,830
Yes, oh, but enter through the side.

316
00:13:19,830 --> 00:13:22,560
Batman is a load-bearing blanket.

317
00:13:28,090 --> 00:13:29,550
Amazing.

318
00:13:29,550 --> 00:13:30,800
I know.

319
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:32,280
This isn't the printout.

320
00:13:32,280 --> 00:13:34,550
This is my real face.

321
00:13:35,890 --> 00:13:36,990
What are we doing?

322
00:13:36,990 --> 00:13:38,070
This is a fort.

323
00:13:38,070 --> 00:13:39,780
Let's sit on the floor.

324
00:13:40,590 --> 00:13:42,940
Roughing it? Okay.

325
00:13:46,790 --> 00:13:49,920
Everything looks so big from down here.

326
00:13:51,370 --> 00:13:54,620
This must be how Leonard feels.

327
00:13:57,540 --> 00:13:59,060
I'm surprised you could hear me

328
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:01,590
with this Thin Mint in your ear!

329
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:04,180
Yeah, pretty cool, huh?

330
00:14:04,180 --> 00:14:05,660
Yeah.

331
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:08,650
Anyway, I should probably get going.

332
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:11,330
Howie, have you noticed how often people say that

333
00:14:11,330 --> 00:14:13,500
when you start to do magic?

334
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:16,730
Sorry, I have a long drive.

335
00:14:16,770 --> 00:14:19,730
Well, I hope I get to see you again.

336
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:21,520
I hope so, too.

337
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:24,920
I've always dreamed of having a brother to play catch with.

338
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:27,380
Keep dreaming.

339
00:14:28,170 --> 00:14:30,500
Hey, I threw out the first pitch at an Angels game.

340
00:14:31,260 --> 00:14:33,810
He did it with a robot.

341
00:14:34,860 --> 00:14:37,540
You had sex with a robot?

342
00:14:38,380 --> 00:14:39,700
That's not what she meant.

343
00:14:39,700 --> 00:14:41,770
But technically, yes.

344
00:14:44,190 --> 00:14:46,200
Hang on, if you're making all this money,

345
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:47,190
where is it?

346
00:14:47,190 --> 00:14:48,420
In a safe place.

347
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:50,050
What does that mean, under your bed?

348
00:14:50,050 --> 00:14:53,820
No, it means a diversified portfolio of stocks and bonds.

349
00:14:53,820 --> 00:14:54,990
I'm not overly conservative.

350
00:14:54,990 --> 00:14:58,720
I'm young, so my guy said I can afford to take some risks.

351
00:14:59,210 --> 00:15:01,480
Wait a minute, you have "a guy"?

352
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:02,730
Don't you have a guy?

353
00:15:02,730 --> 00:15:05,590
Why would I have a guy? I don't have any money!

354
00:15:06,930 --> 00:15:07,840
Oh, sweetie,

355
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:10,160
you should really get some money.

356
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,490
For those of you listening at home,

357
00:15:12,490 --> 00:15:15,460
how great is this?

358
00:15:15,930 --> 00:15:18,400
Wil, I'm begging you, just please turn that off.

359
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:19,930
Sure.

360
00:15:21,250 --> 00:15:23,940
And we're back.

361
00:15:24,440 --> 00:15:26,470
Leonard, why are you making such a big deal out of this?

362
00:15:26,470 --> 00:15:29,040
So our roles have changed a bit over the last couple years.

363
00:15:29,040 --> 00:15:30,520
This is the way life is.

364
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:33,350
And I'm sure in time they'll change again.

365
00:15:33,350 --> 00:15:36,000
Great, you're not only more successful than me,

366
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,200
now you're more mature.

367
00:15:38,550 --> 00:15:40,330
Okay, look, would it make you feel better

368
00:15:40,330 --> 00:15:43,720
if I did something dumb like sneak out of work one day

369
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:46,210
to go audition for a Kevin Smith movie?

370
00:15:46,210 --> 00:15:48,540
That would be great, thank you!

371
00:15:49,610 --> 00:15:51,200
I'm just gonna jump in here real quick.

372
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:52,780
Leonard, a moment ago,

373
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:55,750
you were dead set against Penny resuming her acting career,

374
00:15:55,750 --> 00:15:57,240
but now you're all for it.

375
00:15:57,240 --> 00:16:00,720
Is it fair to say that she played you like a violin?

376
00:16:02,030 --> 00:16:04,060
Yes, it is, Wil.

377
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:09,340
So, I have to ask,

378
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:11,590
was the robot sexy?

379
00:16:12,140 --> 00:16:16,340
It was actually just a mechanical hand.

380
00:16:16,760 --> 00:16:18,970
'Cause that's all you need, right?

381
00:16:20,510 --> 00:16:22,380
You are my brother.

382
00:16:24,220 --> 00:16:26,690
Okay, so the final four forts

383
00:16:26,690 --> 00:16:29,630
in the first annual best fort ever contest,

384
00:16:29,630 --> 00:16:31,460
or "Fort off," are...

385
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:33,700
...Fort Knox,

386
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:35,340
Fort Ticonderoga,

387
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:37,800
Fort Sumter and...

388
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:40,180
Fort Cozy McBlanket.

389
00:16:41,450 --> 00:16:42,610
I'd say...

390
00:16:42,610 --> 00:16:44,520
Knox over Ticonderoga

391
00:16:44,520 --> 00:16:45,830
'cause it's got the gold.

392
00:16:45,830 --> 00:16:47,910
And McBlanket over Sumter

393
00:16:47,910 --> 00:16:50,050
'cause it has a higher thread count.

394
00:16:53,790 --> 00:16:55,320
10:00.

395
00:16:55,790 --> 00:16:56,880
Date night's over.

396
00:16:56,880 --> 00:16:58,110
Wait, no.

397
00:16:58,110 --> 00:17:00,020
We haven't picked a winner.

398
00:17:00,020 --> 00:17:02,130
We both know this one's gonna win.

399
00:17:02,130 --> 00:17:03,840
Well, of course we do.

400
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:08,000
Fort Knox doesn't have a secret physics lending library.

401
00:17:09,530 --> 00:17:11,390
Come on, I'll help you take this down.

402
00:17:11,390 --> 00:17:12,210
Well, wait.

403
00:17:12,210 --> 00:17:14,330
What if, just this once,

404
00:17:14,330 --> 00:17:16,680
we suspend the date night parameters

405
00:17:16,680 --> 00:17:18,980
and you stay later?

406
00:17:20,860 --> 00:17:24,390
Well, as long as we're suspending the parameters,

407
00:17:25,290 --> 00:17:26,670
I could stay really late

408
00:17:26,670 --> 00:17:29,620
and we could have our first sleepover.

409
00:17:30,900 --> 00:17:32,870
That's a big step.

410
00:17:32,870 --> 00:17:35,010
It's a big fort.

411
00:17:37,890 --> 00:17:38,830
Very well.

412
00:17:38,830 --> 00:17:41,890
I will agree to a family-friendly,

413
00:17:41,890 --> 00:17:45,400
G-rated, boy-girl sleepover.

414
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:47,760
Pg.

415
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:51,350
Some scenes may be too intense for younger viewers.

416
00:17:52,210 --> 00:17:53,520
G-rated,

417
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:57,340
with a warning for families with babies and toddlers.

418
00:17:59,100 --> 00:18:01,010
You got yourself a sleepover.

419
00:18:02,110 --> 00:18:04,920
Do you need to borrow a toothbrush or pajamas?

420
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:06,660
Would it alarm you to know

421
00:18:06,660 --> 00:18:09,170
that I hid those things here two years ago

422
00:18:09,170 --> 00:18:11,580
just in case this ever came up?

423
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:14,920
It would,

424
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:17,700
but you know how much I admire preparedness.

425
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:21,420
How did you know we'd be in the living room?

426
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:24,060
Who says this is the only one I hid?

427
00:18:34,690 --> 00:18:36,760
Hello? What is this?

428
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:38,690
We built a fort!

429
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:41,460
Are those my sheets?

430
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:43,150
Yes, they are.

431
00:18:44,250 --> 00:18:45,130
Okay.

432
00:18:45,130 --> 00:18:47,200
Well, you kids have fun.

433
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:48,140
I'm gonna go to sleep.

434
00:18:48,480 --> 00:18:50,710
Leonard, don't you want to see the inside of the fort?

435
00:18:50,710 --> 00:18:51,780
Yeah, I'm good.

436
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:53,090
Oh, come on.

437
00:18:53,090 --> 00:18:55,630
I really wanted to tell someone they can't come in.

438
00:18:56,630 --> 00:18:57,780
Okay, fine.

439
00:18:57,780 --> 00:19:01,150
Sheldon, may I please visit your fort?

440
00:19:01,150 --> 00:19:02,040
I want to say no,

441
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:04,370
but it's too glorious. Get in here!

442
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:07,970
Thank you.

443
00:19:07,970 --> 00:19:09,810
Okay, have a seat on the floor.

444
00:19:09,810 --> 00:19:12,300
Not there. That's my spot.

